{"id": "Dd_XwNKTwZ6", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "Why would a god wear a deadly snake around his neck? 🐍\n\nThe answer begins with poison.\n\nWhen the devas and asuras churned the Ocean of Milk, Vasuki, king of serpents, became their rope. From the ocean rose Halahala—a poison so deadly it could destroy the world.\n\nEveryone ran.\n\nShiva did not.\n\nHe drank the poison to save creation. Parvati held his throat to stop it from spreading, turning it blue—earning him the name Neelkanth, the Blue-Throated One.\n\nIn many tellings, Shiva later honoured Vasuki by giving him a place around his neck, close to where the poison had stopped.\n\nTo us, a snake can represent fear, death and the passage of time.\n\nShiva doesn’t run from any of them.\n\nHe wears them.\n\nParvati, too, once feared the power within her. How Shiva helped her master it—and how she became Maa Chandraghanta, the Fierce Protector—is the story we tell in Book 3 of our Navadurga picture-book series, created for ages 4–8.\n\n📚 Get all 3 books for ₹600 at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\n💬 What fear would YOU learn to wear?\n\n📌 Save this for Navratri storytime, and share it with someone who loves Shiva’s stories.\n\n#Shiva #Mahadev #Neelkanth #Vasuki #SamudraManthan HarHarMahadev HinduMythology IndianMythology Navratri Navadurga Chandraghanta PictureBooks KidsBooks MythologyForKids RishiKatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1790940376, "like_count": 5515, "comment_count": 16, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1785982725884358a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 60.674, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr2-2.fna.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQPafc2ght30bi7Tcoi8ixLWKEOpB3c_S1u8xrKyz4wJvnVWTluTfVxciXSAMQVASHTSr5uAg__PvSHx4i-mn13z5Ha-Q_wFbCifQtk.mp4?_nc_cat=104&_nc_oc=Adrnd6uP